<h1>Why Do Companies Say Entry-Level Then Require 2 Years Experience?</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">Companies call roles "entry-level" because they want junior salaries, then demand experience because they have no reliable way to filter for actual skill. The two-year ask is a proxy for "someone else already vetted you," not proof you can do the work, and the whole thing collapses once you show scored proof instead of a thin resume.</p>

<div class="tldr">
<ul>
<li>The "entry-level" label with experience requirements is <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">experience inflation</a>: 46% of employers dropped degree requirements on paper, but fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected real change, so they defaulted to time-based proxies instead.</li>
<li>Two years of "experience" functions as a risk filter, not a skill measure: it means another employer already absorbed the cost of training you, so the new company doesn't have to.</li>
<li>Internships cut underemployment odds by <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">48.5%</a> precisely because they provide that proxy signal, which is why new-grad hiring at big tech has collapsed more than 50% from 2019 while internship pipelines remain fiercely competitive.</li>
<li>The bypass is not "get experience" faster, it is replacing the proxy with direct evidence: scored work samples that prove you can execute before day one.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<section>
<h2>Where did the "entry-level" label come from?</h2>

<p>Once upon a time, entry-level meant exactly what it said: you enter, they train you. Companies built structured programs, assigned mentors, and accepted that your first six months would be net negative productivity. That system died for three reasons, and understanding them makes the current absurdity obvious.</p>

<p>First, the job market flooded. When degree completion rose and the pipeline swelled, employers stopped needing to invest. They could demand candidates who already had the training, and someone would show up. Second, the tools got more complex faster than curricula adapted, so a fresh graduate's "relevant coursework" often meant a semester of theory three software versions behind. Third, and most importantly, recruiting scaled through automation. Applicant tracking systems don't evaluate potential, they filter on keywords. "2 years experience" is a clean boolean flag that cuts a thousand-resume pile to something manageable.</p>

<p>The result is a linguistic bait-and-switch that everyone pretends not to notice. The company writes "entry-level" because that is the compensation band, the org-chart slot, the expectation they want to set for salary and autonomy, then they append "2+ years" because they have no infrastructure to assess whether you can actually perform, and time served feels safer than betting on potential. It is not malice. It is a system that ran out of ways to distinguish signal from noise, so it defaulted to the crudest available proxy.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Why two years specifically?</h2>

<p>Two years is not a magic number. It is approximately long enough that someone else has already trained you, you have survived at least one performance cycle, and you have accumulated enough war stories to sound credible in an interview. It is the point where a new employer can plausibly claim you are "pre-vetted" and therefore lower-risk.</p>

<p>This is the filter operating system at work: the same logic that runs universities, now colonizing hiring. Just as a degree certifies you sat through four years and were not expelled, two years of experience certifies you showed up somewhere else and were not fired. Neither guarantees skill, and both are scarcity filters dressed up as qualifications.</p>

<p>The cruelty is asymmetric. Experienced candidates suffer too, but they at least have the proxy. New graduates and career-switchers are caught in a catch-22 that the job posting itself created: you need experience to get experience, and the "entry-level" role that should break the cycle demands you have already escaped it. The market is not actually short of capable people. It is short of mechanisms to identify them.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>What are companies actually afraid of?</h2>

<p>Hiring is expensive and firing is harder. The average cost-per-hire for a professional role runs into thousands of dollars in recruiter time, interview hours, and onboarding before you produce anything. A bad hire is worse: sunk cost, team disruption, legal exposure if termination gets contested. Companies are not evil for wanting to reduce this risk. They are just using a terrible method.</p>

<p>The two-year experience requirement is essentially a transfer of training cost. Employer A invests in making you productive; Employer B poaches you and free-rides on that investment. This is why industries with high poaching rates, like technology and finance, have the most inflated experience requirements even for nominally junior roles. It is also why companies fiercely guard internship pipelines: those are the rare spaces where they still accept the training burden, because the conversion rate to full-time hire, <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/">63.1% for 2024-25</a>, justifies the upfront cost.</p>

<p>What companies fear is not inexperience itself, but the inability to distinguish between someone who will ramp quickly and someone who will never ramp at all. Given that uncertainty, they prefer the devil they know: a candidate whose previous employer already made the bet and survived.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>How do you bypass the paradox?</h2>

<p>The honest answer is not "grind for two years at Starbucks and call it project management." That is playing a broken game by broken rules. The structural answer is to make the proxy unnecessary by delivering what the proxy was supposed to guarantee: evidence that you can execute.</p>

<p>This is where the resume-as-document dies. A resume is a claim. "Proficient in Python" means nothing. "Led cross-functional team" means nothing. Every entry-level candidate has the same bullet points, differentiated only by the prestige of the institution that issued them, and when everyone optimizes the same document, the document becomes noise.</p>

<p>The replacement is scored proof of work: actual tasks completed against a professional bar, with feedback attached, visible to the employer before they ever interview you. Not a certificate that says you watched videos. Not a badge that says you completed a simulation. Work that was evaluated, that you iterated on, that demonstrates the specific judgment the role requires. This is what the <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers using skills-based hiring in 2025</a> claim to want, though most still lack the infrastructure to evaluate it systematically.</p>

<p>The opportunity is for candidates to bring the infrastructure with them. When you arrive with scored, comparable work samples, you have preemptively answered the question the two-year requirement was proxying for, de-risking yourself so the employer no longer needs someone else's vetting because they can see your vetting directly.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Why this will keep getting worse before it gets better</h2>

<p>Experience inflation is a coordination problem with no individual fix. No single company can unilaterally drop the two-year requirement and trust other signals, because other signals are weak. The result is a ratchet: once three companies in an industry demand two years, the fourth must follow or get swamped with unscreenable applicants. The equilibrium is collectively irrational but individually stable.</p>

<p>The only force that breaks such equilibria is a better coordination mechanism. In hiring, that means a standardized, trusted way to evaluate skill directly, at scale, without relying on time-served proxies. This is why skills-based hiring keeps getting announced and keeps stalling: <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">46% of employers dropped degree requirements</a>, but only 31% followed through with real operational change, and the actual hire-through rate was negligible. The intention is there. The infrastructure is not.</p>

<p>What changes the game is not another corporate pledge but a candidate-side shift. When enough candidates arrive with independently verified, scored work, employers can finally hire on proof instead of pedigree, and the two-year requirement becomes irrelevant not because HR reforms itself, but because the candidates who bypass it outperform the ones who don't. Filter systems die not by decree, but by obsolescence.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>Should I apply to "entry-level" jobs that require 2 years experience anyway?</h3>
<p>Yes, but with a different application. Send proof of work, not just a resume. The posting is a wishlist, not a contract. Your goal is to make the hiring manager override the filter by showing them something the filter was supposed to find.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>Does freelance or contract work count as "experience"?</h3>
<p>It counts if you can prove what you did. Unverifiable freelance claims are treated as resume padding, while scored, evaluated work is treated as evidence. The distinction is proof, not the employment label.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>Why don't companies just train people anymore?</h3>
<p>They do, but selectively and expensively. Structured new-grad programs survive at elite companies because they can attract talent that justifies the investment. Everyone else free-rides on someone else's training or demands pre-trained candidates. The training did not disappear; it got hoarded.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>Is this worse in some industries than others?</h3>
<p>Yes. Tech, finance, and consulting have the worst inflation because they have the most applicants and the most poaching. Roles with licensure requirements, like nursing or accounting, have clearer entry paths because the credential itself functions as proof. The chaos is concentrated where credentials are weakest and competition is highest.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
<h3>What is zero's approach to this?</h3>
<p>zero builds scored proof of work in company-shaped scenarios. You complete tasks that mirror actual roles, get evaluated against a professional bar, and accumulate evidence that replaces the experience proxy. Recruiters see your scores, not your years.</p>
</div>
</section>

